**Attribution under Section 5 is a standing condition of any consent granted.**
Consent to build, publish or commercialise a Derivative Application never
removes the attribution obligation unless the Licensor waives it expressly and
in writing.

## 5. Attribution

### 5.1 When attribution is required

You must display the Attribution Notice, in the manner set out in Section 5.3,
whenever You do any of the following:

1. Distribute, publish or make available the Software or any part of it, under
   consent granted per Section 4;
2. Publish, distribute, host or make available **any Derivative Application**;
3. Use the **Curated Data**, or any substantial part of it, in any other
   software, dataset, publication or product — including its selection,
   arrangement, corrections and groupings, whether or not You reformat it;
4. Reproduce any protected expression of the Software elsewhere. This includes
   its structure, sequence and organisation; its module and schema design; its
   algorithms as expressed here; its comments, identifiers and documentation;
   and its user-interface text.

For the avoidance of doubt, Sections 5.1(2)–(4) apply **whether or not** the
result is written in the same programming language, uses the same framework, or
looks the same. Porting, transpiling, machine-translating or having a model
rewrite the Software or the Curated Data produces a Derivative Application and
carries the same obligation.

### 5.2 The Attribution Notice

The Attribution Notice is the following text, reproduced without alteration
except that You may translate it, provided the name, the URL and the sense are
preserved:

```
Based on Japanese Practice by Mensura Media (メンスラ・メディア).
https://github.com/MensuraMedia/language-learning-flashcards
Used under the Japanese Practice Personal Use Licence.
```

Where only the Curated Data is used, "Based on" may be replaced with
"Character data and groupings derived from".

### 5.3 Where the notice must appear

The Attribution Notice must be **reasonably discoverable by an ordinary user of
Your work**, not buried where only a developer would find it. Specifically, it
must appear in **all** of the following that exist for Your work:

| Surface | Requirement |
|---|---|
| **The running application** | In an "About", "Credits", "Acknowledgements" or equivalent screen reachable from the ordinary interface, in legible text of a size and contrast comparable to surrounding body text |
| **Source repository** | In the `README` (or equivalent front page) and in a `NOTICE`, `CREDITS` or `LICENSE` file |
| **Source files** | In the header of any file that contains copied or adapted material |
| **Distribution listing** | In the description or credits of any store, package index or download page from which Your work is obtained |
| **Documentation** | Wherever third-party components or sources are credited |

The notice must not be styled, positioned, coloured or animated so as to make
it effectively invisible, and it must not be presented in a way that implies the
Licensor endorses, sponsors or is affiliated with Your work.

### 5.4 Attribution is not a substitute for permission

Displaying the Attribution Notice **does not grant You any rights**. It is a
condition attached to rights, not a source of them. Building a Derivative
Application still requires prior express written consent under Section 4;
attributing the Licensor while doing so without consent is a breach of both
Section 3 and this Section.

### 5.5 Scope and limits of this Section

Stated plainly, so that this Section is not read to claim more than it does:

1. This Section binds a person who has accepted this Licence in order to obtain,
   run or study the Software. It is a condition of the permission granted in
   Section 2.
2. It does **not** apply to a work created **independently**, without access to
   or reference to the Software or the Curated Data. Two people can arrive at a
   flash-card application honestly, and this Licence makes no claim over that.
3. Copyright does not protect ideas, concepts, methods of operation, facts, or
   the Japanese language. The Licensor claims **no ownership** of spaced
   repetition, of multiple-choice drilling, of the kana or kanji themselves, or
   of the JLPT levels. What is claimed is this expression of them, and this
   compilation of that data.
4. Nothing in this Section restricts discussing, describing, reviewing,
   teaching about, criticising, benchmarking or reporting on the Software, and
   no attribution obligation arises from doing so.
5. If any part of this Section is unenforceable in Your jurisdiction, the
   remainder continues to apply, per Section 11.

